#
# prima-materia — the pylons, and the boat that asks its own name
#
# The third instalment of the names of the Book of the Dead, and the one that
# changes what the first two were evidence for. traditions/egyptian-names.ttl
# holds the forty-two of chapter 125 and traditions/egyptian-arits.ttl the
# twenty-one officers of chapter 147. Both are gates. The reading behind this
# file went to chapter 146 for the pylon doorkeepers it had promised, and
# found on the way that the same examination is put by a boat.
#
# Chapter 99, of bringing along a boat in the underworld. Twenty-two parts of
# the vessel speak in turn and each says tell me my name: the wood, the
# rudder, the rope, the oar-rests, the hold, the mast, the lower deck, the
# upper post, the sail, the pieces of leather, the paddles, the matchabet, the
# planks, the bows, the hull, the rudder again, the keel, the sailor, the wind
# that bears him, the river, the river banks, and the ground he would walk on.
# Every one is answered in the same breath as it asks. The chapter then closes
# with the ordinary knowledge rubric.
#
# That is the finding and it is worth more than the names it yielded. What
# pmc:AegyptiaPortaeNomina described at the hall of the two Maati is not a
# property of doorways. It is this corpus's general form for anything the dead
# man must get past or must use, and it is applied to tackle and timber with
# the same seriousness as to a god. Nothing in the chapter distinguishes the
# keel from the doorkeeper: both ask, both are answered, and the answer is in
# the book the buyer was sold.
#
# What could be entered as names, and why so little of it. The boat chapter
# answers almost every part with a descriptive phrase which Budge gives in
# English — Leg of Hapiu, Standard of Ap-uat, Throat of Mestha, Hair with
# which Anpu finisheth the work of my embalmment. An identifier formed from
# those would be formed from a translation, which AGENTS.md forbids. The one
# set answered with transliterated names is the eight planks of the hull, and
# those eight are here. The rest of the chapter is held as the concept below.
#
# The pylons of chapter 146 gave nine of the ten they have. Budge prints the
# chapter from the Papyrus of Nu, and only the first ten pylons name a
# doorkeeper at all; from the eleventh the formula changes and the doorkeeper
# disappears. Of those ten the first is lost in the scan this run used: the
# page reads the name of thy doorkeeper and then breaks into the plate. Nine
# are entered and the tenth is named as missing rather than guessed.
#

pmc:AegyptiaPylonorumNomina a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    rdfs:label "The secret pylons: what is known at each, and where the doorkeeper stops"@en, "Die geheimen Pylone: was an jedem gewusst wird, und wo der Pförtner aufhört"@de ;
    skos:definition "The form of chapter 146. At each pylon of the house of Osiris in Sekhet-Aanru the dead man says that he has made his way, that he knows the pylon, that he knows its name, and that he knows the name of the god who guards it. The pylon's name is then given as a long epithet — lady of tremblings with lofty walls, fire the lady of flames, blazing fire whose flame cannot be quenched — and after it, for the first ten pylons only, the name of the doorkeeper."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter has two halves and the seam is at the eleventh pylon. Up to the tenth the dead man knows the name of the god who guardeth you and a doorkeeper is named. From the eleventh he knows instead the name of her who is within thee, no doorkeeper is named at all, and every pylon closes with the same fixed clause: she hath the judgment of the feeble bandaged one. Two different things have been joined under one chapter number, and the join is visible in the wording."@en ;
    skos:note "How many pylons there are depends on the manuscript, and Budge says so in a footnote at the eleventh: the Papyrus of Ani enumerates ten only, the version Naville published has twenty vignettes, and the Turin papyrus gives fifteen. The chapter as printed runs to twenty-one. This is the same finding as the one at pmp:EgyptianAssessorNameSearch in another place — the number is a property of the copy and not of the thing counted."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 146, Papyrus of Nu (British Museum No. 10,477, sheet 25), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 464-472" .

pmp:AegyptiaNavisNomina a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    rdfs:label "Bringing along the boat: answering every part of it by name"@en, "Das Boot herbeiführen: jedem seiner Teile den Namen sagen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The operation of chapter 99. After an address to those who bring the boat over the back of Apep, the parts of the vessel put their question one after another, each in the same words — tell me my name, or tell us our names — and each is answered. The sequence runs through the wood, the rudder, the rope, the oar-rests, the hold, the mast, the lower deck, the upper post, the sail, the pieces of leather, the paddles, the matchabet, the planks of the hull, the bows, the hull, the rudder a second time, the keel, the sailor, the wind, the river, the river banks and the ground. A closing address to the divine beings with beautiful Kas is then recited before them."@en ;
    skos:note "The examination is put by the equipment. The corpus applies to a rope and a plank the form it applies at the gate of the underworld, and it draws no distinction of kind between them: the keel asks in the same words as the doorkeeper and is answered out of the same book."@en ;
    skos:note "The rudder asks twice and is answered differently each time — Leg of Hapiu at the first asking, Aqa, the true one, at the second. Nothing in the chapter marks the repetition as an error or as two different rudders."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 99, Papyrus of Nu (British Museum No. 10,477, sheets 21 and 22), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 297-302" .

pmc:AegyptiaNavisRubrica a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    rdfs:label "He shall come forth into Sekhet-Aarru, and the followers of Horus shall reap for him"@en, "Er wird hervorgehen nach Sechet-Aaru, und die Gefolgsleute des Horus werden für ihn ernten"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the rubric of chapter 99 says the knowing of it obtains. If this chapter be known by the deceased he shall come forth into Sekhet-Aarru; bread and wine and cakes shall be given unto him at the altar of the great god; and fields and an estate sown with wheat and barley, which the followers of Horus shall reap for him. He shall eat of that wheat and barley, his limbs shall be nourished therewith, his body shall be like unto the bodies of the gods, and he shall come forth into Sekhet-Aarru in any form whatsoever he pleaseth, regularly and continually."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition is knowing and not doing, as at pmc:AegyptiaRubricaScientiae, and the chapter it attaches to is a list of names. What is promised is an estate with other people working it, which is the most concrete thing any rubric read in this corpus promises and the one least available to inspection."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 99, rubric, Papyrus of Nu (British Museum No. 10,477, sheets 21 and 22), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 301-302" .

pmc:AegyptiaNavisEfficacia a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    rdfs:label "Knowing the names of the boat yields the estate in Sekhet-Aarru"@en, "Das Wissen der Namen des Bootes bringt das Gut in Sechet-Aaru hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AegyptiaNavisNomina ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AegyptiaNavisRubrica ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual ;
    skos:note "Entered as the claim of the transmission. No term, no sign, no stated way for it to fail, and no case. The one thing this claim has which most in the corpus do not is a named labour force: the followers of Horus reap the barley, and the man who knows the chapter eats it."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 99, rubric, Papyrus of Nu (British Museum No. 10,477, sheets 21 and 22), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 301-302" .

pmc:AegyptiaExaminatioRerum a pm:Systematizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    rdfs:label "The naming examination is the corpus's general form, not the form of its gates"@en, "Die Namensprüfung ist die allgemeine Form des Korpus, nicht die Form seiner Tore"@de ;
    skos:definition "Four places in the corpus put the same examination, and only two of them are gates. At the hall of the two Maati every element of the doorway refuses passage unless told its name. At the seven arits three officers are named at each. At the secret pylons a god and, for the first ten, a doorkeeper are named. And in chapter 99 the parts of a boat ask, one after another, to be told their names. The common form is exact: the thing addressed asks, the answer is supplied in the same passage, and the knowing of the whole is what the attached rubric rewards."@en ;
    skos:note "Systematizing rather than generalizing, and the distinction matters here. This is one tradition's arrangement of its own material, put together from four places in one corpus, and it is asserted as the shape of that corpus and of nothing else. A claim about transmissions in general would need witnesses outside this one and a counter-search behind it; that is the work at generalizations/ and it is not done here."@en ;
    skos:note "The compiler named below is this project and not an Egyptian. The four places are the corpus's; putting them in one row is an act of this holding, dated, and the class exists so that a reader is not left to mistake the row for a testimony."@en ;
    skos:note "What this settles about the first two files. The forty-two and the twenty-one were entered as the names of gatekeepers. They are better read as instances of a procedure the corpus applies to whatever the dead man must get past or use, gods and rigging alike, and the procedure is the object worth holding. See pmc:AegyptiaRubricaScientiae for what the knowing is said to obtain and pmc:AegyptiaPortaeNomina for the one place where the failure is stated."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "prima-materia" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-19" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapters 99, 125, 146 and 147, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 297-302, 375-377, 464-472 and 473-479" .

#
# The pylon doorkeepers, second to tenth
#

pmc:AegyptiaMesPeh a pm:Naming ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    rdfs:label "Mes-Peh"@en, "Mes-Peh"@de ;
    pm:nameForm "mes-peh" ;
    pm:nameRole "doorkeeper of the second pylon of the house of Osiris in Sekhet-Aanru" ;
    skos:altLabel "Mes-Ptah"@en ;
    skos:definition "The doorkeeper of the second of the pylons of chapter 146. The dead man says that he knows the pylon, its name and the name of the god who guards it; the pylon's own name is then given as a long epithet, and the doorkeeper's name is given last and alone."@en ;
    skos:note "Budge prints Mes-Peh (or Mes-Ptah); the edition gives both readings and decides between them nowhere."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 146, the second pylon, Papyrus of Nu (British Museum No. 10,477, sheet 25), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 464-472" .

